---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ExcelScreenshot from "../../components/ExcelScreenshot.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BaseLayout title="Build a transposition tool in Excel">
  <header class="wrap page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">GUIDE 01 / EXCEL</p>
    <h1>
      Build a transposition
      <br />
      tool in Excel<span class="accent">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p>
      Start with your first formula. Build a letter grid, move its columns, and copy the decoded
      message.
    </p>
    <div class="guide-meta">
      <span>NO EXCEL EXPERIENCE NEEDED</span>
      <span>MICROSOFT 365 / EXCEL 2024</span>
    </div>
  </header>
  <div class="wrap article-layout">
    <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="Guide contents">
      <p class="eyebrow">IN THIS GUIDE</p>
      <a href="#basics">01 / Your first formula</a>
      <a href="#references">02 / Follow a cell reference</a>
      <a href="#inputs">03 / Prepare the text</a>
      <a href="#letters">04 / Extract letters</a>
      <a href="#grid">05 / Make the grid</a>
      <a href="#move">06 / Move columns</a>
      <a href="#output">07 / Join the answer</a>
      <a href="#refresh">08 / Use another message</a>
    </nav>
    <article class="guide-content">
      <p>Transposition changes the order of letters. Our original demo ciphertext is:</p>
      <pre>TSEML ELVEO ERTST</pre>
      <p>
        We will arrange it in five columns, then move those columns into order{" "}
        <strong>5, 3, 1, 2, 4</strong>. The result will read <strong>LET’S MOVE LETTERS</strong>.
        You can use the same sheet to try other messages and key lengths.
      </p>
      <p>
        Work through one small experiment at a time. Each screenshot shows the selected cell’s
        address at the top left and its formula in the bar above the grid. Click a screenshot to
        open it at full size.
      </p>

      <h2 id="basics">1. Find a cell and type your first formula</h2>
      <p>
        Open a blank workbook. Each square is a <strong>cell</strong>. Columns have letters across
        the top; rows have numbers down the left. <strong>B3</strong> is where column B meets row 3.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Double-click the sheet tab at the bottom and rename it <strong>Basics</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Click B3. Check that the small box to the left of the formula bar says <strong>B3</strong>
          .
        </li>
        <li>
          Type <code>=1+2</code>, then press Enter.
        </li>
        <li>
          Click B3 again. The cell shows <strong>3</strong>, but the formula bar shows{" "}
          <strong>=1+2</strong>.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        name="formula"
        alt="Excel with B3 selected: the formula bar shows equals 1 plus 2 and the cell displays 3."
        caption="The selected cell contains an instruction. The grid displays its answer."
      />
      <p>
        A <strong>formula</strong> starts with <code>=</code>. Excel works out the instruction and
        displays the result. A plain number or a piece of text does not need an equals sign. To
        replace a cell’s contents, select it and type. To edit its existing formula, select the cell
        and click in the formula bar, or press F2.
      </p>

      <h2 id="references">2. Follow a reference, then try copying it</h2>
      <h3>First, read the value in one other cell</h3>
      <p>
        Type <strong>10</strong> in A3. Replace the formula in B3 with:
      </p>
      <pre>=A3+1</pre>
      <p>
        Read this as “take the value in A3 and add one”. A3 contains 10, so B3 displays 11.{" "}
        <code>A3</code> is a <strong>cell reference</strong>: it tells Excel where to look.
      </p>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        name="reference"
        alt="Excel with A3 containing 10 and B3 displaying 11; the formula bar shows equals A3 plus 1."
        caption="B3 reads A3. The formula bar shows where the answer comes from."
      />
      <p>
        <strong>Try it:</strong> change A3 to 20. B3 changes to 21 without you editing its formula.
        Change A3 back to 10 before continuing.
      </p>

      <h3>Next, copy the formula down one row</h3>
      <p>
        Type <strong>20</strong> in A4. We want to do the same calculation for that row.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Select B3 and press Ctrl+C.</li>
        <li>Select B4 and press Ctrl+V.</li>
        <li>Press Escape to finish copying, then select B4 and look at the formula bar.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        B4 contains <code>=A4+1</code> and displays 21. Excel changed A3 to A4 because the formula
        moved down one row. B3 still reads the cell to its left; B4 now does the same.
      </p>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        name="copied"
        alt="Excel with B4 selected and formula equals A4 plus 1. A3 is 10, A4 is 20, and the answers in B3 and B4 are 11 and 21."
        caption="Copying down changed the reference from A3 to A4. Compare the formula bar with the previous screenshot."
      />
      <p>
        Excel calls this a <strong>relative reference</strong>. It is useful when each row should
        use its own input. You do not need to memorise that name yet; just notice what changed when
        you copied.
      </p>

      <h3>Finally, keep the source cell fixed</h3>
      <p>
        Sometimes every copied formula should read the <em>same</em> input. Our letter tool will
        need this: every extracted letter must come from the same ciphertext cell.
      </p>
      <p>In B3, change the formula to:</p>
      <pre>=$A$3+1</pre>
      <p>
        Copy B3 to B4 again. Select B4 and check the formula bar. It still says <code>=$A$3+1</code>
        . Both answers are now 11 because both formulas read A3, even though A4 contains 20.
      </p>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        name="fixed"
        alt="Excel with B4 selected and formula equals dollar A dollar 3 plus 1. B3 and B4 both display 11 while A4 still contains 20."
        caption="The dollar signs kept the source cell A3 fixed when the formula was copied."
      />
      <p>
        <code>$A</code> keeps column A fixed; <code>$3</code> keeps row 3 fixed. Together,{" "}
        <code>$A$3</code> means “keep reading this exact cell when copied”. Excel calls this an{" "}
        <strong>absolute reference</strong>.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Check your understanding:</strong> change A3 to 30. Both B3 and B4 should become
          31. The dollar signs fix the address, not the value stored there. Put A3 back to 10
          afterwards.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="inputs">3. Prepare the ciphertext in small steps</h2>
      <p>
        Use the + beside the sheet tabs to add three sheets. Double-click their tabs to name them{" "}
        <strong>Inputs</strong>, <strong>Arrange</strong> and <strong>Readout</strong>. Spelling
        matters because formulas will use these names.
      </p>
      <p>
        On Inputs, enter these labels and values. A label is just ordinary text to help you
        recognise an input.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Label in column A</th>
            <th>Cell</th>
            <th>Enter in column B</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Key length</td>
            <td>B2</td>
            <td>
              <code>5</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Ciphertext</td>
            <td>B3</td>
            <td>
              <code>TSEML ELVEO ERTST</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Now add the following formulas <strong>one at a time</strong>, checking each result before
        moving on. A <strong>function</strong> is a named instruction. Its inputs go inside
        parentheses, separated by commas. Text inside a formula needs double quotation marks.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Label in column A</th>
            <th>Cell</th>
            <th>Formula</th>
            <th>Expected result</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Remove spaces</td>
            <td>B4</td>
            <td>
              <code>=SUBSTITUTE(B3," ","")</code>
            </td>
            <td>
              <code>TSEMLELVEOERTST</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Uppercase</td>
            <td>B5</td>
            <td>
              <code>=UPPER(B4)</code>
            </td>
            <td>
              <code>TSEMLELVEOERTST</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Letter count</td>
            <td>B6</td>
            <td>
              <code>=LEN(B5)</code>
            </td>
            <td>15</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Grid rows</td>
            <td>B7</td>
            <td>
              <code>=ROUNDUP(B6/B2,0)</code>
            </td>
            <td>3</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        <code>SUBSTITUTE</code> reads B3, finds a space (<code>" "</code>), and replaces it with
        nothing (<code>""</code>). <code>UPPER</code> reads the result from B4 and capitalises it.{" "}
        <code>LEN</code> reads B5 and counts its characters.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a <strong>chain</strong>: one formula’s answer becomes the next formula’s input.
        Notice that B5 reads B4, not the original text in B3.
      </p>
      <p>
        B7 divides the letter count by the key length. <code>ROUNDUP(...,0)</code> rounds up to a
        whole number of rows. Fifteen letters fit in three rows of five; sixteen letters would need
        a fourth row.
      </p>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        name="inputs"
        alt="Excel Inputs sheet with the demo ciphertext, key length 5, cleaned text, 15 letters and 3 rows. B4 is selected and the formula bar shows SUBSTITUTE."
        caption="Select B4 to see the space-removal formula. Check B5, B6 and B7 as you build the chain."
      />
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>
            Use one line containing A–Z letters and ordinary spaces for this first version.
          </strong>{" "}
          This simple cleanup does not remove punctuation, tabs or line breaks. Use a positive whole
          number for the key length. These formulas use English function names and commas; some
          Excel settings use semicolons instead.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="letters">4. Take one letter, then make a list</h2>
      <p>On Basics, try this in an empty cell:</p>
      <pre>=MID("HELLO",2,1)</pre>
      <p>
        The result is E. <code>MID</code> takes some text, a starting position and the number of
        characters to take. Positions begin at 1. Change 2 to 4 and you get L.
      </p>
      <p>
        Back on Inputs, type <strong>Position</strong> in D1 and <strong>Letter</strong> in E1. In
        D2, enter:
      </p>
      <pre>=SEQUENCE(B6)</pre>
      <p>
        B6 contains 15, so this writes the numbers 1 to 15 down column D. One formula has filled
        several cells. Excel calls this a <strong>spill</strong>. Keep the cells below D2 empty so
        the answers have room to appear.
      </p>
      <p>In E2, first try:</p>
      <pre>=MID($B$5,D2,1)</pre>
      <p>
        It takes one character from B5, at the position in D2. The answer is T. <code>$B$5</code> is
        the fixed source cell you practised earlier: every letter comes from that same cleaned text.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now add a <code>#</code> after D2:
      </p>
      <pre>=MID($B$5,D2#,1)</pre>
      <p>
        <code>D2#</code> means “all the spilled answers starting at D2”. MID now reads the whole
        list of positions and writes one letter beside each number. To edit a spilled formula,
        select its first cell. If you see <code>#SPILL!</code>, clear anything blocking its results.
      </p>

      <h2 id="grid">5. Turn the list into a grid</h2>
      <p>Leave F empty as a gap. In G1, enter:</p>
      <pre>=SEQUENCE(1,B2)</pre>
      <p>
        This makes <strong>one row</strong> with B2 columns. With key length 5, the headings 1 to 5
        appear in G1:K1.
      </p>
      <p>In G2, enter:</p>
      <pre>=WRAPROWS(E2#,B2,"")</pre>
      <p>
        <code>WRAPROWS</code> takes the list of letters from E2#, puts B2 letters in each row, and
        uses empty text for any unused cells in a short final row. For the demo, you should see
        TSEML, ELVEO and ERTST across three rows.
      </p>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        name="grid"
        alt="Excel showing positions in D, extracted letters in E and a five-column letter grid in G to K. G2 is selected and its WRAPROWS formula is visible."
        caption="The intermediate list is on the left of the grid. Select G2 to check how the rows are made."
      />
      <p>
        Changing the key length later changes the width of this source grid automatically. WRAPROWS
        and TOCOL require Microsoft 365 or Excel 2024. If Excel says <code>#NAME?</code>, check the
        spelling and version.
      </p>

      <h2 id="move">6. Fix the letters as values, then move columns</h2>
      <p>
        The generated grid is a formula result. Before moving its letters, make a separate copy
        containing only their values.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Select Inputs!G1:K4: the five numbered headings and the three data rows.</li>
        <li>Copy, switch to Arrange and select A1.</li>
        <li>
          Choose <strong>Home → Paste ▼ → Values</strong>, using the 123 icon.
        </li>
        <li>
          Select A2. Its formula bar should show the letter <strong>T</strong>, with no equals sign.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        name="before"
        alt="Excel Arrange sheet with original column numbers 1 to 5 and fixed ciphertext letters. A2 is selected; the formula bar contains the plain letter T."
        caption="The formula bar confirms that these are fixed letters, ready to move."
      />
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Paste Values freezes the results.</strong> Freeze Panes is a different feature: it
          keeps headings visible while scrolling. It does not freeze formulas. Leave the source grid
          on Inputs intact.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        We want the original-number headings to end up in order <strong>5, 3, 1, 2, 4</strong>. This
        takes two column moves:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          On Arrange, click the <strong>E</strong> column letter to select the whole column. Point
          to the selection border, hold <strong>Shift</strong>, and drag to just before column A.
          Release at the insertion line. The numbered headings should now be{" "}
          <strong>5, 1, 2, 3, 4</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Original column <strong>3</strong> is now in Excel column D. Select D and Shift-drag it to
          just before column B. The headings should now be <strong>5, 3, 1, 2, 4</strong>.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        If dragging is awkward, select the source column, press Ctrl+X, right-click the destination
        column letter and choose <strong>Insert Cut Cells</strong>. This inserts the moved column
        instead of overwriting another one.
      </p>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        name="arranged"
        alt="Excel Arrange sheet after moving columns: headings 5 3 1 2 4, with LETSM, OVELE and TTERS across the rows."
        caption="Read across each row, then continue on the next: LET’S MOVE LETTERS."
      />
      <p>
        Follow the numbered headings you pasted, not Excel’s fixed column letters A to E. Those
        letters describe positions on the sheet.
      </p>

      <h2 id="output">7. Join the visible letters into one answer</h2>
      <p>
        First try a tiny example on Basics: put <strong>LETS</strong> in A7 and{" "}
        <strong>MOVE</strong> in B7. In C7, enter:
      </p>
      <pre>=TEXTJOIN("",TRUE,A7:B7)</pre>
      <p>
        The answer is LETSMOVE. <code>TEXTJOIN</code> joins cells together. Its first input is the
        separator (empty text here); TRUE ignores empty cells. The colon in <code>A7:B7</code> means
        “from A7 through B7”.
      </p>
      <h3>Describe the rectangle we want to read</h3>
      <p>
        On Readout, type <strong>Grid address</strong> in A2. In B2, type this as ordinary text,
        without an equals sign:
      </p>
      <pre>Arrange!A2:E4</pre>
      <p>
        <code>Arrange!</code> names the sheet. <code>A2:E4</code> is its rectangle of letters,
        excluding the headings. Keeping this address as text lets us read the{" "}
        <em>current positions</em> after moving columns. Ordinary references can change to follow
        the cells being moved.
      </p>
      <p>In B3, try:</p>
      <pre>=INDIRECT(B2)</pre>
      <p>
        <code>INDIRECT</code> reads the cells named by the address in B2. You should see a copy of
        the arranged grid spill from B3. Keep its result area empty while trying this step.
      </p>
      <h3>Read the rectangle across rows</h3>
      <p>Replace the formula in B3 with:</p>
      <pre>=TOCOL(INDIRECT(B2),1,FALSE)</pre>
      <p>
        The inner part, <code>INDIRECT(B2)</code>, gives the same grid as before. <code>TOCOL</code>{" "}
        turns it into one column. The 1 ignores blanks; FALSE reads <strong>across each row</strong>
        , then continues on the next. The letters should start L, E, T, S, M, O, V, E.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now put <strong>Copy the value in D3</strong> in D2. In D3, enter:
      </p>
      <pre>=TEXTJOIN("",TRUE,B3#)</pre>
      <p>
        You already know both parts: TEXTJOIN joins the cells, and B3# means the whole spilled list.
        D3 should display <strong>LETSMOVELETTERS</strong>. Copy D3 alone. Use{" "}
        <strong>Paste Values</strong> when pasting into another spreadsheet; paste normally into a
        text editor or cipher tool.
      </p>
      <ExcelScreenshot
        name="readout"
        alt="Excel Readout sheet with the grid address, a vertical list of letters and LETSMOVELETTERS in selected cell D3. The formula bar shows TEXTJOIN."
        caption="The final answer follows the displayed column order. D3 is the cell to copy."
      />
      <h3>Make the address follow the key length</h3>
      <p>
        Our first address was typed by hand. To make it adjust automatically, try this in an empty
        cell on Basics:
      </p>
      <pre>=ADDRESS(4,5,4)</pre>
      <p>
        The answer is E4: row 4, column 5. The last 4 asks for an address without dollar signs. Our
        grid’s last row is its data-row count plus one heading row; its last column is the key
        length.
      </p>
      <p>Replace the text in Readout!B2 with:</p>
      <pre>="Arrange!A2:"&amp;ADDRESS(Inputs!B7+1,Inputs!B2,4)</pre>
      <p>
        <code>Inputs!B7</code> reads a cell on Inputs. <code>&amp;</code> joins text fragments. For
        our demo, this formula still produces the text <code>Arrange!A2:E4</code>, so the answer
        stays the same. It will now adjust when the key length or row count changes.
      </p>

      <h2 id="refresh">8. Try another message</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Change Inputs!B2 to a positive whole-number key length and B3 to your new ciphertext.
        </li>
        <li>
          Check B7 for the data-row count. Select the new source grid with exactly B2 columns and B7
          + 1 rows, including its headings.
        </li>
        <li>
          Clear the previous values on Arrange, then paste the new grid as <strong>Values</strong>{" "}
          into A1. This prevents old letters remaining when the new message is shorter.
        </li>
        <li>Move the numbered columns into a new order. Readout reads their current positions.</li>
        <li>Check that the joined answer has the same number of letters as Inputs!B6.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Try <code>ABC DEF GH</code> with key length 3. You need three data rows; the last is short.
        The unused cell should be skipped in the joined answer. This sheet treats every row as an
        independent block. A cipher written down columns needs a different reconstruction.
      </p>
      <p class="small">
        The screenshots are actual Excel window captures, showing the formula bar, row and column
        headings, cell lines and sheet tabs. Microsoft references:{" "}
        <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-gb/excel/move-or-copy-cells-rows-and-columns">
          moving columns
        </a>
        ,{" "}
        <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/overview-of-formulas-in-excel-ecfdc708-9162-49e8-b993-c311f47ca173">
          formulas and references
        </a>{" "}
        and{" "}
        <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/wraprows-function-796825f3-975a-4cee-9c84-1bbddf60ade0">
          WRAPROWS
        </a>
        .
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${base}/tools/#transposition`}>
          Open the transposition tool ↗
        </a>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}/decks/cipher-analysis/`}>
          Explore cipher patterns →
        </a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
